    {
      "external_id": "C12-CHEM-CH19-19.7-C-TYPES-OF-ORGANIC-REACTIONS",
      "chapter": "pk-punjab/xii/chemistry/c12-chem/ch19",
      "topic": "pk-punjab/xii/chemistry/c12-chem/ch19/u-c-types-of-organic-reactions",
      "title": "C. Types of Organic Reactions",
      "language": "en",
      "source_refs": [
        {
          "source_document_id": "2d720c54-503b-58ec-b5b1-3307f222f40c",
          "pdf_from": 47,
          "pdf_to": 50,
          "note": "C. Types of Organic Reactions: a main subheading of 19.7 Understanding Organic Reactions, its own page (owner 2026-11-04); PDF 47-50."
        }
      ],
      "body": {
        "blocks": [
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "The reactions of organic compounds can be classified into four main types:"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 4,
            "text": "i. Substitution Reactions"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "In substitution reactions, two reactants react in such a way that an atom or group of atoms in one molecule is replaced by another atom or group of atoms. Two products may be formed. For example:"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{bromoethane}}{\\ce{CH3-CH2-Br}} + \\ce{KOH_{(aq)} ->[H2O]} \\underset{\\text{ethanol}}{\\ce{CH3-CH2-OH}} + \\ce{KBr}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃–CH₂–Br (bromoethane) + KOH(aq) → [H₂O] CH₃–CH₂–OH (ethanol) + KBr"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 4,
            "text": "ii. Addition Reactions"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "In addition reactions, two reactants react to produce a single product. For example:"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{ethene}}{\\ce{CH2=CH2}} + \\ce{H2 ->[Ni][\\text{200–300 °C}]} \\underset{\\text{ethane}}{\\ce{CH3-CH3}}",
            "text_alt": "CH₂=CH₂ (ethene) + H₂ → [Ni, 200–300 °C] CH₃–CH₃ (ethane)"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{propene}}{\\ce{CH3-CH=CH2}} + \\ce{H-Br ->} \\underset{\\text{bromopropane}}{\\ce{CH3-CH2-CH2-Br}}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃–CH=CH₂ (propene) + H–Br → CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–Br (bromopropane)",
            "book_errors": [
              "CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–Br",
              "CH3-CH2-CH2-Br"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "correction",
            "title": "Book correction",
            "text": "By Markovnikov’s rule the main product of propene with HBr is 2-bromopropane, CH₃–CHBr–CH₃, not CH₃–CH₂–CH₂–Br (1-bromopropane); the book’s own Quick Check 19.6 gives CH₃CHBrCH₃."
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 4,
            "text": "iii. Elimination Reactions"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "In elimination reactions, removal of atoms or groups of atoms from two adjacent atoms in the substrate molecule occurs to form a multiple bond. For example:"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{bromoethane}}{\\ce{CH3-CH2-Br}} + \\ce{KOH ->[\\text{alcohol}]} \\underset{\\text{ethane}}{\\ce{CH2=CH2}} + \\ce{KBr + H2O}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃–CH₂–Br (bromoethane) + KOH → [alcohol] CH₂=CH₂ (ethane) + KBr + H₂O",
            "book_errors": [
              "(ethane)",
              "\\text{ethane}"
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "callout",
            "tone": "correction",
            "title": "Book correction",
            "text": "CH₂=CH₂ is ethene, not ethane."
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Elimination reactions are regarded as reverse of addition reactions."
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 4,
            "text": "iv. Hydrolysis"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "In hydrolysis, a compound breaks into two on reaction with water often helped by H⁺ or OH⁻ as catalyst. For example,"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{ethyl ethanoate}}{\\ce{CH3-C(=O)-O-CH2-CH3}} + \\ce{H2O ->[H+]} \\underset{\\text{ethanoic acid}}{\\ce{CH3-C(=O)-OH}} + \\underset{\\text{ethanol}}{\\ce{CH3-CH2-OH}}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃–C(=O)–O–CH₂–CH₃ (ethyl ethanoate) + H₂O → [H⁺] CH₃–C(=O)–OH (ethanoic acid) + CH₃–CH₂–OH (ethanol)"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 4,
            "text": "v. Condensation Reactions"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "In condensation reactions two molecules combine to form a bigger molecule with or without loss of a smaller molecule like H₂O, NH₃, HCl etc. For example,"
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{propanone}}{\\ce{CH3-C(=O)-CH3}} + \\underset{\\text{hydroxylamine}}{\\ce{H-NH-OH}} \\ce{->} \\ce{CH3-C(OH)(CH3)-NH-OH} \\ce{->} \\underset{\\text{propanoneoxime}}{\\ce{CH3-C(CH3)=N-OH}} + \\ce{H2O}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃–C(=O)–CH₃ (propanone) + H–NH–OH (hydroxylamine) → CH₃–C(OH)(CH₃)–NH–OH → CH₃–C(CH₃)=N–OH (propanoneoxime) + H₂O"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 4,
            "text": "vi. Oxidation"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Loss of electron is called oxidation. Moreover, if a molecule gains oxygen or loses hydrogen or there is increase in number of bonds of carbon with oxygen or increase in oxidation state of carbon, it is oxidized."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{propene}}{\\ce{CH3-CH=CH2}} \\ce{->[O3][Zn/H2O]} \\underset{\\text{ethanal}}{\\ce{CH3-C(=O)-H}} + \\underset{\\text{methanal}}{\\ce{H-C(=O)-H}}",
            "text_alt": "CH₃–CH=CH₂ (propene) → [O₃, Zn/H₂O] CH₃–C(=O)–H (ethanal) + H–C(=O)–H (methanal)"
          },
          {
            "type": "heading",
            "level": 4,
            "text": "vii. Reduction"
          },
          {
            "type": "paragraph",
            "text": "Reduction is a reaction where gain of electron, loss of oxygen or gain of hydrogen, or a decrease in the number of bonds of carbon with oxygen or decrease in oxidation state of carbon occurs."
          },
          {
            "type": "equation",
            "latex": "\\underset{\\text{ethene}}{\\ce{CH2=CH2}} + \\ce{H2 ->[Ni]} \\underset{\\text{ethane}}{\\ce{CH3-CH3}}",
            "text_alt": "CH₂=CH₂ (ethene) + H₂ → [Ni] CH₃–CH₃ (ethane)"
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  ]
}
